  "strategy": "Introduce a new formulation based on the dual function, which provides a lower bound on the objective and smooths the optimization landscape. This method combines a probability vector \\( p = (p_0, p_1, \\dots, p_{n-1}) \\) (simplex-normalized) with a transformed sequence \\( a = (a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_{n-1}) \\) such that \\( a_i = p_i \\cdot h \\) (for some heuristic scaling factor \\( h \\)). The optimization targets minimizing \\( \\max_k (p * p)_k \\) via dual projection, then carefully restricts the search space by exponentiating and normalizing the constraints. The search is accelerated using a combined gradient and penalty method.\n\nThe algorithm proceeds in four phases, incorporating implicit reboots on time boundaries and using the evaluator for authoritative objective comparisons:\n\n1. **Initialization and Early Validation**:\n   - Start from the parent sequence (if available) or a uniform random initialization of dimension 800.\n   - Compute an early dual bound to establish a target improvement trajectory.\n\n2. **Phase 1**: Probability Vector Optimization (600 seconds)\n   - Treat the sequence as a probability vector \\( p \\) (simplex-normalized) on the infinitesimal scale.\n   - Compute the dual function \\( d(p) = 2n \\cdot (p * p)_{\\text{max}} \\cdot \\sum p^2 \\) (note the reduction factor).\n   - Use a projected gradient method to minimize \\( d(p) \\) with bounds \\( ||p||=1 \\), \\( p_i \\ge 0 \\).\n\n3. **Phase 2**: Heuristic Solution Generation (300 seconds)\n   - After optimization stabilizes, scale the probability vector \\( p \\) by a user-defined constant \\( c \\) to generate a solution candidate \\( a = c \\cdot p \\) (without simplex normalization).\n   - Evaluate \\( a \\) using the evaluator and store the minimum observed objective.\n\n4. **Phase 3**: Dimension Sweep (400 seconds)\n   - Explicitly explore multiple dimensions by generating sequences from the simplex vector \\( p \\) via a linear resampling function.\n   - For each dimension \\( n' \\), scale \\( p \\) uniformly and evaluate using the previous methods' intuition.\n\nKey innovations:\n- **Dual Formulation**: The dual function \\( d(p) \\) offers a theoretically grounded objective that's reducible via linear algebra.\n- **Probability-Preserving**: Early phase ensures constraint-compliant sequences and stability.\n- **Efficient Normalization**: Resampling pivots predictably and is measurable with the system clock.\n\nSimulate progress by tracking a variable budget split across phases. Ensure all proposals are evaluated to fit the [0,1000] unconstraint and sum\u22650.01. Return the best authority-based sequence found by any phase, with fallback object if evaluator clips.",
